TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.12', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Attitudes', title: 'Attitudes', short: 'Triadic model; origins; prejudice and stereotyping; persuasion and cognitive dissonance',
  summary: 'Attitudes decide whether people take up sport, stick with it or drop out. Learn the triadic model (cognitive, affective, behavioural), why attitudes can be inconsistent, where attitudes and prejudices come from, and how coaches can change negative attitudes using persuasive communication and cognitive dissonance.',
  spec: [
    'Attitudes, inconsistencies and prejudices in sporting situations',
    'Components of attitudes: triadic model (cognitive, affective and behavioural), with sporting examples',
    'Origins of attitudes: prejudice and stereotyping',
    'Attitude change: methods of changing attitudes from negative to positive, e.g. cognitive dissonance and persuasion',
    'Factors affecting the effectiveness of cognitive dissonance and persuasion'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'What is an attitude?', html: `
<p>An <b>attitude</b> is a learned, relatively stable predisposition to respond in a particular way — positively or negatively — towards an <b>attitude object</b> (a person, activity, place or idea), e.g. towards fitness training, a referee, or women’s football.</p>
[[d:triadic]]
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it is</th><th>Example: attitude towards swimming</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cognitive</b></td><td>beliefs and knowledge</td><td>“Swimming is good for fitness and low impact.”</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Affective</b></td><td>feelings and emotions</td><td>“I enjoy being in the water.”</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Behavioural</b></td><td>how the person actually behaves (intentions and actions)</td><td>“I swim twice a week.”</td></tr></table></div>
<p><b>Inconsistency:</b> the components do not always match. Someone may believe exercise is healthy (cognitive) but dislike it (affective) and never do it (behavioural). This is why attitudes are poor predictors of behaviour, and why changing only beliefs (e.g. telling people exercise is healthy) often fails.</p>` },
    { h: 'Where attitudes come from; prejudice and stereotyping', html: `
<p>Attitudes are <b>learned</b> through socialisation and experience:</p>
<ul><li><b>Past experience</b> — enjoyable PE lessons create positive attitudes; being picked last or humiliated creates negative ones.</li>
<li><b>Significant others</b> — parents, peers, teachers and coaches (social learning); family traditions.</li>
<li><b>Media and role models</b>; culture; <b>conditioning</b> (rewards and punishments).</li></ul>
<p><b>Prejudice</b> is a pre-judgement — a fixed, usually negative attitude towards a group, often based on <b>stereotypes</b> (over-simplified, generalised beliefs, e.g. “netball is a girls’ game”). Prejudice can lead to discrimination (see 1.18) and to people avoiding certain activities.</p>` },
    { h: 'Changing attitudes', html: `
<p><b>Persuasive communication</b> (Hovland) — changing someone’s attitude by persuading them. Its effectiveness depends on:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>More effective when…</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>The persuader</b></td><td>high status, expert, credible, trustworthy, likeable, similar to the audience (a respected coach, a role model)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The message</b></td><td>clear, balanced (both sides), repeated, relevant, backed by evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The recipient</b></td><td>willing to listen; the attitude is not deeply held; lower self-esteem people are more easily persuaded</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The situation</b></td><td>relaxed, informal, with others changing too; positive reinforcement follows</td></tr></table></div>
<p><b>Cognitive dissonance</b> (Festinger) — creating <b>conflict</b> between the components of an attitude causes psychological discomfort (dissonance). To remove it, the person changes one component, and the attitude shifts. A coach can:</p>
<ul><li>give <b>new information</b> (cognitive): “You burn more calories playing badminton than you think”;</li>
<li>create <b>enjoyable experiences</b> (affective): fun, success, a friendly group;</li>
<li>encourage <b>new behaviour</b> (behavioural): a taster session, a different role, modified rules.</li></ul>
<p>Dissonance is more effective when the new information comes from a credible source, the person values the issue, the conflict is large enough to cause discomfort, and the new behaviour is rewarded.</p>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'A teenager says “Gym is boring and I’m no good at it”. Explain how a teacher could use cognitive dissonance to change this attitude. (4 marks)', s: ['Identify the components: affective (“boring”), cognitive (“I’m no good”), behavioural (avoids gym).', 'Create conflict in the cognitive component: show her fitness test improvements or explain the benefits for her netball.', 'Create conflict in the affective component: make sessions enjoyable — music, friends, circuit variety — so she enjoys them.', 'The mismatch between “gym is boring” and her actual enjoyment/success causes dissonance, so she changes her attitude to restore consistency.'], a: 'Create conflict between components; change one to shift the attitude.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Describing the triadic model without a sporting example for each component.', 'Saying attitudes are innate — they are learned.', 'Explaining persuasion without naming the factors (persuader, message, recipient, situation).', 'Confusing prejudice (attitude) with discrimination (action).'],
  cards: [
    ['Attitude?', 'A learned predisposition to respond positively or negatively to an attitude object.'], ['Triadic model?', 'Cognitive (beliefs), affective (feelings), behavioural (actions).'], ['Why are attitudes poor predictors of behaviour?', 'The components may be inconsistent.'],
    ['Origins of attitudes?', 'Past experience, significant others, media, culture, conditioning.'], ['Prejudice?', 'A fixed, pre-judged (usually negative) attitude towards a group.'], ['Persuasive communication factors?', 'Persuader, message, recipient, situation.'],
    ['Cognitive dissonance?', 'Conflict between attitude components causes discomfort, so the person changes a component.'], ['What makes a persuader effective?', 'High status, expertise, credibility, likeability, similarity.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: '“I know running improves my health” is the ___ component.', o: ['cognitive', 'affective', 'behavioural', 'somatic'], x: 'Beliefs/knowledge.' },
    { q: '“I love playing hockey” is the ___ component.', o: ['affective', 'cognitive', 'behavioural', 'instrumental'], x: 'Feelings.' },
    { q: 'Cognitive dissonance works by…', o: ['creating conflict between attitude components', 'punishing negative attitudes', 'ignoring beliefs', 'reinforcing current behaviour'], x: 'Discomfort drives change.' },
    { q: 'Persuasion is most effective when the persuader is…', o: ['credible and high status', 'unknown', 'unqualified', 'hostile'], x: 'Credibility matters.' },
    { q: 'Attitudes are…', o: ['learned', 'innate', 'unchangeable', 'purely physical'], x: 'Through socialisation and experience.' },
    { q: 'A generalised belief that “boys don’t dance” is a…', o: ['stereotype', 'reinforcement', 'motor programme', 'triadic model'], x: 'Over-simplified belief.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Using an example from sport, describe the triadic model of attitudes. [3]', m: 3, ms: ['cognitive: beliefs/knowledge, e.g. “training improves my rugby”', 'affective: feelings, e.g. “I enjoy weights sessions”', 'behavioural: actions, e.g. attends training three times a week'] },
    { q: 'Explain the factors that affect the effectiveness of persuasive communication in changing an athlete’s attitude to fitness training. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['persuader: high status/expert/credible/likeable (e.g. respected coach)', 'message: clear, balanced, repeated, evidence-based', 'recipient: open to change / how strongly held the attitude is', 'situation: informal, supportive, reinforcement follows'] },
    { q: 'Evaluate methods a coach could use to change a young player’s negative attitude towards training. [6]', m: 6, lv: true, ms: ['identify components of the negative attitude (triadic model)', 'cognitive dissonance: new information (benefits) creates conflict', 'affective: make training fun, varied, successful', 'behavioural: new roles, taster activities, modified practices', 'persuasion by credible significant other / role model; reinforcement of new behaviour', 'limitations: deeply held attitudes, peer influence, needs time; judgement'] }
  ],
  sims: ['attitude'], gens: []
});

TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.13', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Aggression', title: 'Aggression', short: 'Hostile vs instrumental aggression and assertion; instinct, frustration–aggression, cue arousal, social learning; causes and control',
  summary: 'Contact sport is physical, but when does it become aggression? Learn the difference between hostile and instrumental aggression and assertive behaviour, the four theories of aggression, the causes of aggressive acts, and the strategies individuals and organisations use to control them.',
  spec: [
    'Definitions and types of aggression: hostile and instrumental aggression; assertive behaviour; the difference between aggression and assertion',
    'Theories: instinct theory and catharsis; frustration–aggression hypothesis; cue arousal theory; social learning theory — links to sporting examples',
    'Causes of aggression, e.g. poor officiating, temperature, partisan crowd',
    'Managing and controlling aggression: strategies for individuals and organisations'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Aggression and assertion', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Hostile (reactive) aggression</b></td><td>behaviour intended to harm another person, often with anger, where harm is the main goal; usually outside the rules</td><td>punching an opponent after a foul</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Instrumental (channelled) aggression</b></td><td>intent to harm, but as a <b>means to achieve a goal</b> (winning), usually without anger; may be within the rules</td><td>a boxer’s punch; a deliberately hard hit to intimidate a quarterback</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Assertive behaviour</b></td><td>forceful, goal-directed behaviour <b>within the rules</b> with <b>no intent to harm</b>; uses legitimate force</td><td>a strong, legal rugby tackle; a hard shoulder charge in football</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The key differences are <b>intent</b> (to harm or not) and whether behaviour is <b>within the rules</b>. Coaches want assertion, not aggression.</p>` },
    { h: 'Theories of aggression', html: `
[[d:aggression]]
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Key idea</th><th>Criticism</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Instinct theory</b> (Lorenz, Freud)</td><td>aggression is <b>innate</b> — an instinct that builds up and must be released. Sport offers a socially acceptable release: <b>catharsis</b> (a cleansing of pent-up aggression).</td><td>aggression varies between cultures and individuals (it is learned); sport can increase rather than release aggression; no evidence of a “build-up”</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Frustration–aggression hypothesis</b> (Dollard)</td><td>frustration (having a goal blocked) <b>always</b> leads to aggression; aggression then releases the frustration (catharsis). If the aggression is punished, frustration increases further.</td><td>frustration does not always cause aggression; aggression can occur without frustration</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cue arousal theory</b> (Berkowitz, aggressive-cue hypothesis)</td><td>frustration raises arousal and creates a <b>readiness</b> for aggression, but aggression only occurs if an <b>aggressive cue</b> (trigger) is present — e.g. a hockey stick, a bat, a particular opponent, crowd chants, a violent sport</td><td>more realistic, but cues are hard to predict</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Social learning theory</b> (Bandura)</td><td>aggression is <b>learned</b> by observing and imitating significant others (role models), especially when their aggression is <b>reinforced</b> (praised, successful, unpunished)</td><td>explains learning well, but not individual differences in reacting to the same models</td></tr></table></div>` },
    { h: 'Causes of aggression', html: `
<ul><li><b>Frustration</b> — losing, poor personal performance, being fouled.</li><li><b>Poor officiating</b> — inconsistent or unfair decisions.</li><li><b>Temperature and discomfort</b> — heat raises arousal and irritability.</li><li><b>A partisan or hostile crowd</b>; local derbies and rivalry; high stakes (a cup final, relegation).</li>
<li><b>Provocation and retaliation</b>; sledging.</li><li><b>Reinforcement</b> — coaches, teammates or media praising aggressive acts; role models behaving aggressively.</li><li>Personality (Type A, low stability), high arousal, alcohol or drugs, the nature of the sport (contact sports provide cues).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Controlling aggression', html: `
<div class="grid g2"><div class="box"><b class="lbl">Individual strategies</b><ul><li>cognitive: self-talk, imagery, counting to ten, focusing on the task, thought stopping</li><li>somatic: breathing control, progressive muscular relaxation</li><li>avoid provocative situations and cues; walk away</li><li>positive reinforcement of assertive, non-aggressive behaviour; punishment of aggression (substitution, fines)</li><li>give responsibility (captaincy); positive role models; reward fair play</li></ul></div>
<div class="box"><b class="lbl">Organisational strategies</b><ul><li>NGB punishments: yellow/red cards, sin bins, bans, fines, points deductions</li><li>video review (TMO, citing commissioners) to punish acts the referee missed</li><li>rule changes (e.g. high-tackle laws in rugby)</li><li>fair-play awards and campaigns; codes of conduct for players, coaches and parents</li><li>education of coaches and officials; better officiating; stewarding of crowds</li></ul></div></div>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'Use the cue arousal theory to explain why an ice hockey player might strike an opponent with a stick after being fouled. (3 marks)', s: ['Being fouled blocks her goal and causes frustration, which raises arousal.', 'Raised arousal creates a readiness for aggression but does not guarantee it.', 'An aggressive cue — the hockey stick in her hand (or the particular opponent) — triggers the aggressive act.'], a: 'Frustration → arousal → cue triggers aggression.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Calling every hard tackle “aggression” — legal, forceful play without intent to harm is assertion.', 'Confusing hostile and instrumental aggression — instrumental aggression is a means to an end.', 'Stating catharsis as fact — it is disputed.', 'Listing control strategies without saying whether they are individual or organisational, or how they work.'],
  cards: [
    ['Hostile aggression?', 'Intent to harm, with anger, harm as the goal; usually outside the rules.'], ['Instrumental aggression?', 'Intent to harm as a means to a goal; often within the rules.'], ['Assertive behaviour?', 'Forceful, within the rules, no intent to harm.'],
    ['Instinct theory?', 'Aggression is innate, builds up and must be released (catharsis).'], ['Catharsis?', 'Release of pent-up aggression through an acceptable outlet.'], ['Frustration–aggression hypothesis?', 'Frustration always leads to aggression.'],
    ['Cue arousal theory?', 'Frustration raises arousal (readiness); a cue triggers aggression.'], ['Social learning theory of aggression?', 'Aggression learned by observing and imitating reinforced role models.'],
    ['Four causes of aggression?', 'Frustration, poor officiating, high temperature, hostile crowd (also rivalry, reinforcement, provocation).'], ['Two organisational controls?', 'Bans/fines/sin bins; video review; codes of conduct; fair-play awards.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A legal, hard tackle with no intent to injure is…', o: ['assertive behaviour', 'hostile aggression', 'instrumental aggression', 'catharsis'], x: 'Within the rules, no intent to harm.' },
    { q: 'Punching an opponent in anger after a foul is…', o: ['hostile aggression', 'assertion', 'instrumental aggression', 'social facilitation'], x: 'Intent to harm as the goal.' },
    { q: 'Which theory says aggression is released through sport?', o: ['Instinct theory (catharsis)', 'Social learning theory', 'Cue arousal theory', 'Attribution theory'], x: 'Catharsis.' },
    { q: 'Cue arousal theory says aggression needs…', o: ['frustration plus an aggressive cue', 'only an instinct', 'a role model only', 'low arousal'], x: 'Berkowitz.' },
    { q: 'A young player copies a professional who is praised for “passion” after fighting. This supports…', o: ['social learning theory', 'instinct theory', 'frustration–aggression', 'drive theory'], x: 'Imitation of reinforced models.' },
    { q: 'Which is an organisational strategy to reduce aggression?', o: ['Retrospective bans using video evidence', 'Counting to ten', 'Imagery', 'Breathing control'], x: 'NGB action.' },
    { q: 'A criticism of the frustration–aggression hypothesis is that…', o: ['frustration does not always lead to aggression', 'it ignores frustration', 'it says aggression is learned', 'it involves cues'], x: 'Too deterministic.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the difference between instrumental aggression and assertive behaviour. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['instrumental: intent to harm as a means to achieve a goal', 'assertive: forceful but within the rules with no intent to harm'] },
    { q: 'A footballer reacts aggressively when his team is losing a local derby.', parts: [
      { q: 'Identify two possible causes of his aggression. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['frustration from losing / goal blocked', 'hostile/partisan crowd / rivalry of a derby / poor officiating / provocation / temperature'] },
      { q: 'Explain his behaviour using the frustration–aggression hypothesis and cue arousal theory. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['F–A: frustration from blocked goal always leads to aggression', 'aggression releases the frustration (catharsis)', 'cue arousal: frustration increases arousal / readiness for aggression', 'aggression only occurs if a cue is present, e.g. the rival player / crowd'] },
      { q: 'Evaluate strategies that could be used to control aggression in football. [6]', m: 6, lv: true, ms: ['individual cognitive: self-talk, imagery, refocusing', 'individual somatic: breathing, relaxation', 'coach: substitute/remove from situation; reinforce assertion; punish aggression', 'organisational: cards, bans, fines, video review', 'education, codes of conduct, fair-play awards, positive role models', 'limitations: in heat of the moment; inconsistent punishment; media reinforcement; judgement'] }
    ] }
  ],
  sims: ['aggress'], gens: []
});

TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.14', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Social facilitation', title: 'Social facilitation and the audience', short: 'Facilitation and inhibition; drive theory, evaluation apprehension, distraction–conflict, self-presentation; home advantage',
  summary: 'Performing in front of others can lift or sink a performance. Learn how audiences and co-actors facilitate or inhibit performance, Zajonc’s drive theory, evaluation apprehension, distraction–conflict and self-presentation theories, home-field advantage, and strategies to reduce the negative effects of an audience.',
  spec: [
    'Positive and negative effects of the presence of an audience on performance',
    'How co-actors and the audience can facilitate or inhibit performance',
    'Theories of social facilitation: drive theory, evaluation apprehension theory, distraction–conflict theory, self-presentation theory',
    'Home field advantage',
    'Strategies to lessen the negative effects of the presence of an audience'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Facilitation and inhibition', html: `
<p><b>Social facilitation</b> is the positive effect of others on performance; <b>social inhibition</b> is the negative effect. The “others” can be:</p>
<ul><li><b>passive others</b> — an <b>audience</b> watching, or <b>co-actors</b> doing the same task at the same time but not competing (e.g. others running on treadmills);</li>
<li><b>interactive others</b> — competitors and teammates;</li><li><b>social reinforcers</b> — coaches, parents, the crowd cheering.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Zajonc’s drive theory', html: `
[[d:zajonc]]
<p>The mere presence of others increases <b>arousal</b>, which makes the <b>dominant response</b> more likely (as in drive theory, 1.11):</p>
<ul><li><b>Facilitates:</b> experts (autonomous stage) whose dominant response is correct; simple and gross skills; extroverts.</li>
<li><b>Inhibits:</b> novices (cognitive stage) whose dominant response is likely to be wrong; complex and fine skills; introverts.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Other theories', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Key idea</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Evaluation apprehension</b> (Cottrell)</td><td>it is not mere presence but the performer’s perception that they are being <b>judged</b> that raises arousal and anxiety. An expert audience, a selector or a scout causes more anxiety than a crowd who cannot see or does not understand.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Distraction–conflict</b> (Baron)</td><td>an audience is a <b>distraction</b>; the performer experiences <b>conflict</b> between attending to the task and to the audience. This attentional overload raises arousal: it can help simple tasks but harms complex tasks requiring concentration.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Self-presentation</b> (Baumeister)</td><td>performers want to present a <b>positive image</b> to others. For confident experts this motivates them to try harder; for those worried about how they look it causes anxiety and self-consciousness, so they focus on appearance rather than the skill — leading to “choking” (e.g. in front of a supportive home crowd in a final).</td></tr></table></div>` },
    { h: 'Home field advantage', html: `
<p>Teams generally win more at home. Reasons: supportive crowd (social reinforcement, may influence officials), familiarity with the venue and conditions, no travel fatigue, territorial behaviour, routine. However, home crowds can increase <b>evaluation apprehension</b> and self-presentation concerns in high-pressure matches — the <b>home disadvantage</b> in finals and deciding games.</p>` },
    { h: 'Reducing the negative effects', html: `
<ul><li><b>Familiarisation</b> — train with an audience present, play crowd noise, visit the venue.</li><li><b>Selective attention</b> — focus on relevant cues, block out the crowd.</li><li><b>Stress-management</b> — cognitive (imagery, self-talk, mental rehearsal) and somatic (breathing, PMR) techniques.</li>
<li>Build <b>confidence</b> and skill to the autonomous stage; overlearn skills.</li><li>Reduce evaluation — emphasise process/performance goals rather than outcome; the coach reduces the importance of the result.</li><li>Positive feedback and support from teammates and coach.</li></ul>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'Use Zajonc’s drive theory to explain why a crowd improves the performance of an elite weightlifter but harms that of a novice gymnast. (4 marks)', s: ['The presence of an audience increases arousal, making the dominant response more likely.', 'The elite weightlifter is autonomous, so her dominant response is correct; weightlifting is also a simple, gross skill — performance is facilitated.', 'The novice gymnast is in the cognitive stage, so her dominant response is likely to be incorrect.', 'Gymnastics is complex and fine — the extra arousal inhibits her performance.'], a: 'Arousal → dominant response: correct for experts, incorrect for novices.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Treating social facilitation as always positive — it includes inhibition.', 'Confusing co-actors (same task, not competing) with competitors.', 'Describing evaluation apprehension as “the crowd makes them nervous” — it is the perception of being judged.', 'Forgetting to link theory to skill type and stage of learning.'],
  cards: [
    ['Social facilitation?', 'The positive effect of the presence of others on performance.'], ['Social inhibition?', 'The negative effect of others on performance.'], ['Co-actors?', 'Others performing the same task at the same time but not competing.'],
    ['Zajonc’s drive theory?', 'Presence of others ↑ arousal → dominant response more likely.'], ['Evaluation apprehension?', 'Perceived judgement by others raises arousal/anxiety.'], ['Distraction–conflict theory?', 'Audience distracts; conflict between task and audience overloads attention.'],
    ['Self-presentation theory?', 'Concern with how one appears to others affects performance.'], ['Home field advantage reasons?', 'Supportive crowd, familiarity, no travel, influence on officials.'], ['Three strategies to reduce audience effects?', 'Familiarisation, selective attention, stress-management techniques.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'According to Zajonc, an audience increases…', o: ['arousal, making the dominant response more likely', 'relaxation', 'skill level', 'group cohesion'], x: 'Drive theory.' },
    { q: 'A scout watching causes more anxiety than a crowd of strangers. This is best explained by…', o: ['evaluation apprehension', 'catharsis', 'social loafing', 'Hick’s law'], x: 'Perceived judgement.' },
    { q: 'Runners on neighbouring treadmills who are not racing are…', o: ['co-actors', 'competitors', 'an audience', 'social reinforcers'], x: 'Same task, not competing.' },
    { q: 'An audience is most likely to inhibit…', o: ['a novice performing a complex skill', 'an expert performing a simple skill', 'an extrovert in a gross skill', 'an autonomous sprinter'], x: 'Incorrect dominant response.' },
    { q: 'Distraction–conflict theory suggests performance suffers because…', o: ['attention is divided between task and audience', 'the crowd is always hostile', 'arousal falls', 'the performer loafs'], x: 'Attentional overload.' },
    { q: 'Which strategy reduces negative audience effects?', o: ['Training with crowd noise present', 'Increasing the importance of the result', 'Ignoring mental preparation', 'Only practising alone'], x: 'Familiarisation.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the evaluation apprehension theory. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['it is the perception of being judged/evaluated by others (not mere presence)', 'that increases arousal/anxiety — greater with expert/significant audience'] },
    { q: 'Describe three strategies a performer could use to reduce the negative effects of an audience. [3]', m: 3, ms: ['familiarisation / train with audience or crowd noise', 'selective attention / focus on relevant cues', 'stress management: imagery / self-talk / breathing / PMR (also process goals, overlearning)'] },
    { q: 'Discuss how the presence of an audience can affect the performance of different sports performers. Refer to theories of social facilitation. [8]', m: 8, lv: true, ms: ['facilitation (positive) and inhibition (negative)', 'Zajonc: audience ↑ arousal → dominant response', 'helps experts, simple/gross skills, extroverts; hinders novices, complex/fine, introverts', 'evaluation apprehension: perceived judgement (scouts, experts) ↑ anxiety', 'distraction–conflict: attention divided → overload for complex tasks', 'self-presentation: concern with image → choking or extra motivation', 'home advantage vs home disadvantage in high-pressure games', 'strategies to reduce; judgement on which theory best explains'], tag: 'ext' }
  ],
  sims: ['audience'], gens: []
});

TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.15', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Group Dynamics and Leadership', title: 'Group dynamics and cohesion', short: 'Features of a group; Tuckman; task and social cohesion; coordination and motivation losses; Ringelmann and social loafing',
  summary: 'A team of stars is not always a star team. Learn what makes a group, how teams form (forming, storming, norming, performing), task and social cohesion and their link to productivity, why coordination and motivation losses occur, the Ringelmann effect and social loafing — and what coaches do about them.',
  spec: [
    'The nature of a group/team: mutual awareness, common goal and interaction; the 6 Is (interaction, interdependence, interpersonal relationships, identical norms/goals/values, identity and independence)',
    'Formation of groups/teams: forming, storming, norming and performing',
    'Theories of group cohesion: task and social cohesion; group cohesion and group productivity',
    'Problems with group processes: coordination and motivational losses; group size — the Ringelmann effect and social loafing',
    'How groups become cohesive teams; motivational factors within a team; how social loafing can be overcome'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'What makes a group?', html: `
<p>A <b>group</b> is two or more people who <b>interact</b>, are <b>mutually aware</b> of each other and share a <b>common goal</b> and a sense of collective identity. A crowd at a bus stop is not a group; a netball team is.</p>
<p>The specification’s features of a group (the “6 Is”): <b>interaction</b> (communicating and working together), <b>interdependence</b> (relying on each other to succeed), <b>interpersonal relationships</b>, <b>identical norms, goals and values</b>, a shared <b>identity</b> (kit, name, “we”), and a degree of <b>independence</b> — members still have their own roles and contribution.</p>` },
    { h: 'Forming a team: Tuckman', html: `
[[d:tuckman]]
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Forming</b></td><td>members get to know each other, assess each other’s abilities, find out whether they belong; polite, dependent on the leader</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Storming</b></td><td>conflict: members compete for status and roles, challenge the leader; can be the most difficult stage</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Norming</b></td><td>conflict is resolved, roles and norms are agreed; cohesion and co-operation develop</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Performing</b></td><td>the team works together towards the goal; roles are clear; energy goes into performance</td></tr></table></div>
<p>(A fifth stage, <b>adjourning</b>, happens when the group breaks up.) Teams can slip back to storming when new players join or results are poor.</p>` },
    { h: 'Cohesion and productivity', html: `
<p><b>Cohesion</b> is the tendency of a group to stick together and remain united in pursuit of its goals (Carron).</p>
<ul><li><b>Task cohesion</b> — members work together to achieve the team’s goals (co-ordinated tactics, shared commitment to winning).</li>
<li><b>Social cohesion</b> — members like each other and enjoy each other’s company.</li></ul>
<p>Task cohesion is more closely linked to success, especially in <b>interactive</b> sports (basketball, netball, football) where players depend on each other. In <b>co-active</b> sports (a golf team, a relay squad of sprinters) task cohesion matters less. Teams with low social cohesion can still win if task cohesion is high; but high social cohesion with low task cohesion (cliques, “just for fun”) can harm performance.</p>
<p><b>Steiner’s model:</b> $"actual productivity" = "potential productivity" − "losses due to faulty processes"$. Potential productivity is what the team could achieve with its members’ abilities; <b>faulty processes</b> are:</p>
<ul><li><b>Coordination losses</b> — poor timing, communication, tactics or understanding between players (e.g. two players going for the same ball).</li>
<li><b>Motivational losses</b> — individuals not giving full effort (social loafing).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'The Ringelmann effect and social loafing', html: `
[[d:ringelmann]]
<p>The <b>Ringelmann effect</b>: as group size increases, the average performance of each individual decreases (in Ringelmann’s rope-pulling experiment, 8 people pulled less than 8 times one person). Some of the loss is coordination loss, some is motivation loss.</p>
<p><b>Social loafing</b>: individuals reduce their effort when working in a group because their contribution cannot be <b>identified</b> (“hiding in the team”). Causes: low identifiability, lack of confidence (low self-efficacy), belief that others are not trying, low task importance, lack of cohesion, unclear roles, being with low-ability teammates.</p>
<div class="box why"><b class="lbl">Overcoming social loafing</b><ul><li>make individual contributions <b>identifiable</b> and give feedback: stats on tackles made, distance covered (GPS), passes completed;</li><li>give each player a <b>specific role</b> and personal goals;</li><li>praise and reward individual effort (player of the match, credit for “number of tackles made”);</li><li>build cohesion with team-building and shared goals; small groups in training;</li><li>raise self-efficacy; reinforce effort; select players who are motivated.</li></ul></div>` },
    { h: 'Building a cohesive team', html: `
<ul><li>set clear team and individual goals; involve players in setting them;</li><li>team-building activities, social events and shared experiences (training camps);</li><li>keep a stable squad; clear roles; a strong captain and leadership group;</li><li>effective communication; resolve conflicts early; avoid cliques;</li><li>reward team achievement as well as individuals; team identity (kit, rituals, name).</li></ul>` }
  ],
  eqs: [['"actual productivity" = "potential productivity" − "faulty processes"', 'Steiner’s model']],
  worked: [
    { q: 'A rugby coach notices that some forwards appear to “hide” in rucks. Explain the cause and suggest how the coach can overcome it. (4 marks)', s: ['This is <b>social loafing</b> — reduced effort because individual contribution is hard to identify in a large group.', 'The coach can make effort identifiable: use video and GPS to record rucks hit, tackles made and work rate for each player.', 'Give each forward a specific role and personal targets, and give feedback after each match.', 'Praise and reward individual effort (e.g. “work-rate player of the match”), raising motivation and self-efficacy.'], a: 'Social loafing → identify contributions, roles, feedback, rewards.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Confusing coordination losses (organisation) with motivational losses (effort).', 'Saying social cohesion is more important than task cohesion for success.', 'Describing Tuckman’s stages without saying what happens in each.', 'Saying the Ringelmann effect is only about laziness — part of it is coordination loss.'],
  cards: [
    ['Group?', 'Two or more people who interact, are mutually aware and share a common goal/identity.'], ['Tuckman’s stages?', 'Forming, storming, norming, performing (adjourning).'], ['Storming?', 'Conflict over roles and status; challenging the leader.'],
    ['Cohesion?', 'Tendency of a group to stick together in pursuit of its goals.'], ['Task vs social cohesion?', 'Working together to achieve goals vs liking each other.'], ['Steiner’s model?', 'Actual productivity = potential productivity − losses due to faulty processes.'],
    ['Coordination losses?', 'Poor timing, communication or tactics between players.'], ['Motivational losses?', 'Reduced individual effort — social loafing.'], ['Ringelmann effect?', 'Individual performance decreases as group size increases.'],
    ['Social loafing?', 'Reduced individual effort in a group when contribution is not identifiable.'], ['Overcoming social loafing?', 'Identify contributions (stats), specific roles, feedback, rewards, cohesion.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'The stage where team members compete for roles and challenge the leader is…', o: ['storming', 'forming', 'norming', 'performing'], x: 'Conflict.' },
    { q: 'Members working together to achieve the team goal is…', o: ['task cohesion', 'social cohesion', 'social loafing', 'the Ringelmann effect'], x: 'Task focus.' },
    { q: 'Two players collide going for the same catch. This is a…', o: ['coordination loss', 'motivational loss', 'social loafing', 'form of catharsis'], x: 'Faulty process in organisation.' },
    { q: 'The Ringelmann effect states that…', o: ['individual performance decreases as group size increases', 'larger groups always perform better', 'cohesion increases with size', 'leaders emerge in large groups'], x: 'Rope-pulling experiment.' },
    { q: 'Publishing each player’s tackle count helps reduce…', o: ['social loafing', 'task cohesion', 'self-efficacy', 'forming'], x: 'Makes effort identifiable.' },
    { q: 'According to Steiner, actual productivity equals potential productivity minus…', o: ['losses due to faulty processes', 'social cohesion', 'the number of players', 'leadership'], x: 'Coordination and motivation losses.' },
    { q: 'Task cohesion matters most in…', o: ['interactive sports such as basketball', 'individual sports', 'co-active golf teams', 'none'], x: 'Players depend on each other.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the difference between task cohesion and social cohesion. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['task: members working together to achieve the team’s goals', 'social: members liking each other / enjoying each other’s company'] },
    { q: 'Describe Tuckman’s four stages of group formation. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['forming: getting to know each other / assessing abilities', 'storming: conflict / competing for roles / challenging leader', 'norming: roles and norms agreed / cohesion develops', 'performing: working together towards goals / roles clear'] },
    { q: 'Evaluate the causes of faulty processes in a team and the strategies a coach could use to improve team productivity. [8]', m: 8, lv: true, ms: ['Steiner: actual = potential − faulty processes', 'coordination losses: poor timing, communication, understanding, tactics', 'motivational losses: social loafing — contribution not identifiable, low confidence', 'Ringelmann effect: larger groups → lower individual performance', 'strategies for coordination: practise set plays/drills, clear roles, communication', 'strategies for motivation: identify contributions (stats/GPS), feedback, rewards, goal setting', 'build task cohesion: team-building, shared goals, stable squad, captaincy', 'judgement: task cohesion and minimising losses matter more than simply selecting best individuals'], tag: 'ext' }
  ],
  sims: ['loafing'], gens: ['ringel']
});

TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.16', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Group Dynamics and Leadership', title: 'Leadership', short: 'Great man theory; Fiedler; prescribed and emergent leaders; autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire; Chelladurai; LSS',
  summary: 'Who makes a good captain or coach? Learn the great man and contingency theories, prescribed and emergent leaders, the three leadership styles and when each works, Chelladurai’s multi-dimensional model and the Leadership Scale for Sport, and the skills an effective leader needs.',
  spec: [
    'Theories of leadership: great man theory, Fiedler’s contingency theory; prescribed and emergent leaders',
    'Leadership styles: autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire — benefits and weaknesses',
    'Variations of leadership style depending on the situation, member characteristics and the personality of the leader',
    'Chelladurai’s multi-dimensional model of leadership; the Leadership Scale for Sport (LSS)',
    'Skills necessary to be an effective leader, e.g. communication and knowledge of the sport'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Leaders and how they are chosen', html: `
<ul><li><b>Great man theory</b> — leaders are <b>born, not made</b>; leadership qualities are innate traits (often linked to men from powerful backgrounds). Criticism: it ignores learning and the situation; there is no set of traits that guarantees leadership.</li>
<li><b>Prescribed leader</b> — appointed from <b>outside</b> the group by a higher authority (e.g. a national coach appointed by the WRU). Can bring new ideas, but may lack the group’s respect at first, and can cause conflict.</li>
<li><b>Emergent leader</b> — comes from <b>within</b> the group, chosen by the members or rising naturally (e.g. a captain voted by the team). Already respected and knows the group; may struggle to discipline former peers.</li></ul>
<p>Most sport psychologists accept that leadership skills can be <b>learned</b> (social learning) and that effectiveness depends on the situation.</p>` },
    { h: 'Leadership styles', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Style</th><th>What it involves</th><th>Works best when…</th><th>Weaknesses</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Autocratic</b> (task-oriented)</td><td>the leader makes all decisions and gives instructions</td><td>large groups; beginners (cognitive stage); dangerous situations; little time (half-time, a time-out); hostile groups; many members prefer it in team contact sports</td><td>can demotivate experienced players; no ownership; resentment</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Democratic</b> (person-oriented)</td><td>the leader shares decisions and consults the group</td><td>small groups; experienced, intelligent performers; friendly atmosphere; time available (pre-season planning); individual sports</td><td>slow; can lack direction; not suited to emergencies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Laissez-faire</b></td><td>the leader steps back and lets the group make decisions</td><td>highly experienced, motivated, creative groups (elite squads working on tactics)</td><td>lack of direction and structure; group may lose focus; beginners flounder</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The best style depends on <b>the situation</b> (time, danger, size of group, task), the <b>members</b> (age, gender, ability, experience, personality, preferences) and the <b>leader’s own personality</b>.</p>` },
    { h: 'Fiedler’s contingency theory', html: `
[[d:fiedler]]
<p>Leadership effectiveness depends on matching the leader’s style — <b>task-oriented</b> (autocratic) or <b>person-oriented</b> (relationship-focused, democratic) — to how <b>favourable</b> the situation is. Favourableness depends on: leader–member relations (good/poor), task structure (clear/unclear), and the leader’s position power (strong/weak).</p>
<ul><li><b>Highly favourable</b> (good relations, clear task, strong power) or <b>highly unfavourable</b> (poor relations, unclear task, weak power) → a <b>task-oriented</b> leader is most effective.</li>
<li><b>Moderately favourable</b> → a <b>person-oriented</b> leader is most effective.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Chelladurai’s multi-dimensional model and the LSS', html: `
[[d:chelladurai]]
<p><b>Antecedents</b> — situational characteristics, leader characteristics and member characteristics — determine three types of leader behaviour:</p>
<ul><li><b>Required behaviour</b> — what the situation demands;</li><li><b>Actual behaviour</b> — what the leader actually does;</li><li><b>Preferred behaviour</b> — what the group members want.</li></ul>
<p>The <b>consequences</b> (performance and member satisfaction) are best when all three are <b>congruent</b> (match). If actual behaviour differs from what is required, performance suffers; if it differs from what members prefer, satisfaction falls.</p>
<p>The <b>Leadership Scale for Sport (LSS)</b> (Chelladurai and Saleh) is a questionnaire measuring five dimensions of coaching behaviour — <b>training and instruction</b>, <b>democratic behaviour</b>, <b>autocratic behaviour</b>, <b>social support</b> and <b>positive feedback</b> — from the athletes’ perception (actual and preferred) or the coach’s own view.</p>` },
    { h: 'Qualities of an effective leader', html: `
<p>Effective communication (with players and fellow coaches), detailed knowledge of the sport, the ability to motivate, empathy, decision-making, confidence, charisma, organisation, adaptability of style, consistency and fairness, leading by example, and good interpersonal skills.</p>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'Using Chelladurai’s model, explain why a group of experienced hockey players might become dissatisfied with a new coach. (3 marks)', s: ['The players (member characteristics: experienced, skilled) <b>prefer</b> a democratic style with some input into tactics.', 'If the new coach’s <b>actual</b> behaviour is autocratic, it does not match their preferred behaviour.', 'Chelladurai predicts that a mismatch between actual and preferred behaviour reduces member satisfaction (and possibly performance).'], a: 'Actual ≠ preferred behaviour → lower satisfaction.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Saying autocratic leadership is “bad” — it suits particular situations.', 'Confusing prescribed (appointed from outside) and emergent (from within) leaders.', 'Getting Fiedler the wrong way round — task-oriented leaders suit the extremes.', 'Listing Chelladurai’s behaviours without explaining congruence and the consequences.'],
  cards: [
    ['Great man theory?', 'Leaders are born with innate leadership traits.'], ['Prescribed leader?', 'Appointed from outside the group by a higher authority.'], ['Emergent leader?', 'Comes from within the group.'],
    ['Autocratic style suits…', 'Large groups, beginners, danger, little time, hostile groups.'], ['Democratic style suits…', 'Small groups, experienced performers, time available, friendly atmosphere.'], ['Laissez-faire?', 'Leader steps back; group decides — suits highly experienced, motivated groups.'],
    ['Fiedler: task-oriented leaders are best when…', 'The situation is highly favourable or highly unfavourable.'], ['Fiedler: person-oriented leaders are best when…', 'The situation is moderately favourable.'],
    ['Chelladurai’s three leader behaviours?', 'Required, actual, preferred.'], ['When are performance and satisfaction highest?', 'When required, actual and preferred behaviours match.'], ['LSS five dimensions?', 'Training and instruction, democratic, autocratic, social support, positive feedback.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A captain voted for by the team is a…', o: ['emergent leader', 'prescribed leader', 'laissez-faire leader', 'great man'], x: 'From within the group.' },
    { q: 'Which style suits a coach giving instructions in a 60-second time-out?', o: ['Autocratic', 'Democratic', 'Laissez-faire', 'Person-oriented'], x: 'Little time for discussion.' },
    { q: 'Fiedler suggests a person-oriented leader is most effective when the situation is…', o: ['moderately favourable', 'highly favourable', 'highly unfavourable', 'dangerous'], x: 'Middle of the continuum.' },
    { q: 'In Chelladurai’s model, what the group wants is the…', o: ['preferred behaviour', 'required behaviour', 'actual behaviour', 'antecedent'], x: 'Member preferences.' },
    { q: 'The Leadership Scale for Sport measures…', o: ['five dimensions of coaching behaviour', 'trait anxiety', 'group size effects', 'VO₂max'], x: 'Chelladurai and Saleh.' },
    { q: 'A criticism of great man theory is that it…', o: ['ignores learning and the situation', 'ignores genetics', 'is too flexible', 'uses questionnaires'], x: 'Leaders can be made.' },
    { q: 'Laissez-faire leadership is most suitable for…', o: ['experienced, highly motivated groups', 'beginners', 'large, hostile groups', 'dangerous activities'], x: 'They can direct themselves.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the difference between prescribed and emergent leaders. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['prescribed: appointed from outside the group by an external authority', 'emergent: comes from within the group / chosen by the members'] },
    { q: 'Describe Fiedler’s contingency theory of leadership. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['effectiveness depends on leader style and the situation', 'task-oriented vs person-oriented leaders', 'favourableness: leader–member relations, task structure, position power', 'task-oriented best in highly favourable/unfavourable; person-oriented in moderately favourable'] },
    { q: 'A new coach has been appointed to a university netball team.', parts: [
      { q: 'Explain Chelladurai’s multi-dimensional model of leadership. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['antecedents: situation, leader and member characteristics', 'required, actual and preferred behaviour', 'consequences: performance and satisfaction', 'best when all three are congruent / mismatch reduces performance or satisfaction'] },
      { q: 'Evaluate which leadership styles the coach should use with the team. [6]', m: 6, lv: true, ms: ['autocratic: time-outs, match tactics, large squad, disciplinary issues — quick decisions', 'but may demotivate experienced university players', 'democratic: setting goals, planning, experienced/intelligent members — ownership', 'but slow; not in emergencies', 'laissez-faire: only for mature, motivated players in some situations', 'judgement: flexible/adaptive style matched to situation and members (Chelladurai/Fiedler)'] }
    ] }
  ],
  sims: ['leader'], gens: []
});

TOPICS.push({
  id: '3.17', unit: '3', area: 'psych', ref: 'Attribution theory', title: 'Attribution theory and learned helplessness', short: 'Weiner’s model; self-serving bias; actor–observer effect; gender; learned helplessness; attributional retraining',
  summary: 'The reasons people give for winning and losing shape their future motivation. Learn Weiner’s model (locus of causality and stability), common attribution errors — the self-serving bias, the actor–observer effect and gender differences — and how learned helplessness develops and can be reversed with attributional retraining.',
  spec: [
    'Attribution theory: internal/external and stable/unstable reasons given for outcomes; Weiner’s model',
    'Why different reasons are given for outcomes by team members',
    'Errors in attribution: self-serving bias, the actor–observer effect and gender differences',
    'Learned helplessness: causes, its effect on performance and disaffection with sport in young people',
    'Strategies to overcome learned helplessness: attributional retraining',
    'Interpret graphical representations associated with sport psychology theories'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Weiner’s model', html: `
[[d:weiner]]
<p><b>Attributions</b> are the reasons we give for the outcome of an event (success or failure). Weiner classified them on two dimensions:</p>
<ul><li><b>Locus of causality</b> — <b>internal</b> (within the performer: ability, effort) or <b>external</b> (outside: task difficulty, luck, the referee, the weather).</li>
<li><b>Stability</b> — <b>stable</b> (fixed, unlikely to change: ability, task difficulty) or <b>unstable</b> (changeable: effort, luck).</li></ul>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Internal</th><th>External</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Stable</b></td><td>ability</td><td>task difficulty (strength of opposition)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Unstable</b></td><td>effort</td><td>luck</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Weiner later added <b>controllability</b> (whether the performer can control the cause — effort is controllable; ability, luck and task difficulty are not).</p>
<p><b>Why it matters:</b> attributions affect <b>expectations</b> of future success, <b>motivation</b>, <b>pride and shame</b>. Attributing success to <b>internal, stable</b> causes (ability) builds confidence and expectation of success; attributing failure to <b>external or unstable, controllable</b> causes (effort, luck, tactics) protects self-esteem and keeps motivation.</p>` },
    { h: 'Errors and biases', html: `
<ul><li><b>Self-serving bias</b> — attributing success to internal factors (“we were brilliant”) and failure to external factors (“the referee cost us”), to protect self-esteem. Common among high achievers (NAch).</li>
<li><b>Actor–observer effect</b> — performers (actors) attribute their own failure to external, situational factors (the pitch, the wind); observers (coaches, spectators) attribute the same failure to internal factors in the performer (lack of ability or effort). This can cause conflict between coach and player. Team members also give different reasons for the same result depending on their role.</li>
<li><b>Gender differences</b> — traditional research suggested males more often attribute success to ability and failure to bad luck, while females more often attribute success to luck or effort and failure to lack of ability — linked to socialisation and lower expectations; recent research shows this is changing.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Learned helplessness', html: `
<p><b>Learned helplessness</b> (Dweck) is the belief, acquired through repeated failure, that failure is <b>inevitable</b> and <b>beyond one’s control</b> — “whatever I do, I’ll fail”.</p>
<ul><li><b>Global (general)</b> learned helplessness — believing you are bad at all sports.</li><li><b>Specific</b> learned helplessness — believing you are bad at one skill or sport (“I can’t take penalties”).</li></ul>
<p><b>Causes:</b> repeated failure, especially when attributed to <b>internal, stable, uncontrollable</b> factors (“I’ve no ability”); negative feedback from significant others; comparison with more able peers; being selected last; unrealistic goals.</p>
<p><b>Effects:</b> low self-efficacy, low motivation, NAF behaviour, reduced effort, avoidance of challenge, poor performance, and <b>disaffection and drop-out</b> — a major reason young people stop taking part in sport.</p>` },
    { h: 'Attributional retraining', html: `
<p><b>Attributional retraining</b> teaches the performer to change the attributions they make:</p>
<ul><li>attribute <b>failure</b> to <b>internal, unstable, controllable</b> factors such as effort, tactics or technique (which can be improved), or to external unstable factors;</li><li>attribute <b>success</b> to <b>internal, stable</b> factors such as ability.</li></ul>
<p>Supporting strategies: set realistic, achievable goals (success experiences); positive feedback and praise for effort; use Bandura’s sources of self-efficacy; focus on performance/process goals rather than outcomes; mastery climate; avoid comparison with others; small-sided games and modified activities; point out improvements.</p>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'After losing heavily, a young swimmer says “I’m just not a good swimmer”. Explain the dangers of this attribution and how a coach could use attributional retraining. (5 marks)', s: ['“Not good” = <b>lack of ability</b> — an internal, stable, uncontrollable attribution.', 'If repeated, it lowers her expectation of future success, reduces motivation and can lead to <b>learned helplessness</b> and dropping out.', 'Retraining: the coach helps her attribute the loss to <b>effort, tactics or technique</b> — internal but unstable and controllable (“your turns cost you 2 seconds; we can fix them”).', 'Also attribute any success to ability to build confidence.', 'Set achievable goals and praise improvement to raise self-efficacy.'], a: 'Change stable/uncontrollable attributions to unstable/controllable ones.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Putting luck in the wrong box — luck is external and unstable.', 'Saying the self-serving bias is always bad — it protects self-esteem and motivation.', 'Describing learned helplessness without its cause (attributions of repeated failure).', 'Suggesting attributional retraining means blaming external factors for everything.'],
  cards: [
    ['Attribution?', 'The reason given for the outcome of an event.'], ['Weiner’s two dimensions?', 'Locus of causality (internal/external) and stability (stable/unstable).'], ['Internal + stable?', 'Ability.'], ['Internal + unstable?', 'Effort.'], ['External + stable?', 'Task difficulty.'], ['External + unstable?', 'Luck.'],
    ['Controllability?', 'Whether the cause is under the performer’s control (effort is).'], ['Self-serving bias?', 'Success → internal; failure → external, to protect self-esteem.'], ['Actor–observer effect?', 'Actors blame external factors; observers blame the actor’s internal factors.'],
    ['Learned helplessness?', 'Belief that failure is inevitable and uncontrollable, after repeated failure.'], ['Global vs specific helplessness?', 'All sports vs one skill/sport.'], ['Attributional retraining?', 'Changing failure attributions to internal, unstable, controllable causes (effort).']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In Weiner’s model, luck is…', o: ['external and unstable', 'internal and stable', 'external and stable', 'internal and unstable'], x: 'Outside and changeable.' },
    { q: 'Effort is…', o: ['internal, unstable and controllable', 'external and stable', 'internal and stable', 'uncontrollable'], x: 'Can be changed by the performer.' },
    { q: '“We won because we’re the better team, we lost because of the referee.” This is…', o: ['the self-serving bias', 'the actor–observer effect', 'learned helplessness', 'social loafing'], x: 'Protecting self-esteem.' },
    { q: 'A coach blames a player’s lack of effort, while the player blames the wet pitch. This is…', o: ['the actor–observer effect', 'the self-serving bias only', 'catharsis', 'cognitive dissonance'], x: 'Different perspectives.' },
    { q: 'Learned helplessness is most likely when failure is attributed to…', o: ['internal, stable, uncontrollable factors', 'luck', 'effort', 'task difficulty only once'], x: 'E.g. lack of ability.' },
    { q: 'Attributional retraining encourages attributing failure to…', o: ['effort or tactics', 'lack of ability', 'fixed talent', 'nothing'], x: 'Controllable and changeable.' },
    { q: 'Task difficulty is…', o: ['external and stable', 'internal and unstable', 'external and unstable', 'internal and stable'], x: 'e.g. strength of opposition.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Using Weiner’s model, classify the attributions “the opposition were too strong” and “I didn’t try hard enough”. [2]', m: 2, ms: ['opposition too strong: task difficulty — external, stable', 'didn’t try hard enough: effort — internal, unstable (controllable)'] },
    { q: 'Explain the self-serving bias and why performers use it. [3]', m: 3, ms: ['success attributed to internal factors (ability/effort)', 'failure attributed to external factors (luck/referee/task)', 'to protect self-esteem / maintain motivation and confidence'] },
    { q: 'Discuss the causes and effects of learned helplessness in young people and how teachers and coaches can prevent it. [8]', m: 8, lv: true, ms: ['learned helplessness: belief failure is inevitable/uncontrollable', 'caused by repeated failure attributed to internal, stable, uncontrollable factors (lack of ability)', 'negative feedback, comparison, being picked last, unrealistic goals', 'global vs specific', 'effects: low self-efficacy, low motivation, NAF behaviour, avoidance, drop-out/disaffection', 'attributional retraining: failure → effort/tactics; success → ability', 'achievable goals, praise effort, mastery climate, Bandura’s sources of self-efficacy', 'judgement: early intervention in school PE key to lifelong participation'], tag: 'ext' }
  ],
  sims: ['weiner'], gens: []
});
